# Address-block sharded borrow tracker: tile threading stops anti-scaling

date: 2026-08-07
host: Apple M4 Pro (8 P-core + 4 E-core = 12 logical), macOS 26.5.2, aarch64,
      `hw.cachelinesize` = 128, L1D = 128 KiB per P-core
rustc: stable, release profile (lto = "fat", codegen-units = 1), default
       features (bitdepth_8 + bitdepth_16 — the CHECKED DisjointMut build).
       No `-C target-cpu=native`.
branch: perf/shard-tracker, off `probe/shard-sizing` (= main a6a7e23 plus the
        2026-08-07 recon's benchmark records and its inert `__probe_*`
        instrumentation).
tracker commit measured: f01ada8 (32 shards, BLOCK_SHIFT 12)
NOT composed with the open #450 (`perf/audit-445`) row-batching branch — see
"Relationship to #450".

## nice level — read this before reproducing

NO `nice` WAS APPLIED TO ANY TIMED RUN. On Darwin a positive nice value maps the
process to background QoS (efficiency cores) and distorts wall clock by well
over an order of magnitude. `nice -n 19` was used for BUILDS only.

Arms are interleaved back to back within each (vector, threads) cell and their
order is rotated every round; every number below is a MEDIAN OF 9 (3 rounds x 3
reps). `scripts/perf/ab_sweep.sh` refuses to start while any process is above
25% CPU and records the 1-minute load average with every cell. Final run:
252/252 cells, ZERO FAIL rows, load 1.01 / 2.16 / 4.58 (min / median / max).

## The three arms

All three are the SAME commit; only the tracker differs, selected by a cargo
feature. A cross-commit A/B would also carry every other difference between the
trees.

  legacy   `--features tracker-legacy` — the pre-sharding tracker
           (`crates/rav1d-disjoint-mut/src/tracker_legacy.rs`): one `TinyLock`
           plus one 64-slot table per `DisjointMut`. This file is main's
           `mod checked` VERBATIM — verified by diffing the dedented module
           against `git show a6a7e23:.../lib.rs`; the only differences are the
           probe-feature-gated additions (inert here) and the constructor
           signature.
  shard32  default — 32 shards (`N_SHARDS`), 4 KiB blocks (`BLOCK_SHIFT` 12).
  shard64  `--features shards-64`.

Two changes are common to all three arms and so cancel out of the A/B, but do
mean `legacy` is not byte-identical to main: `DisjointMut::tracker` is boxed,
and open-ended `Bounds` (`index(..)` / `index(n..)`, which encode their end as
`usize::MAX`) are clamped to the container length. For orientation against main
itself, `benchmarks/tracker_decomp_2026-08-07.meta` measured main at 586.5 ms
(t=1) and 1264.4 ms (t=8) on v4k_8tile 8bpc; `legacy` here lands at 602.3 and
1678.9. The t=8 convoy cell is intrinsically noisy — that meta records main
spanning 1264-1343 ms across builds and this sweep saw 1679-1718 across two
runs — so treat the t=8 baseline as "somewhere between 1.3 and 1.7 s", not as a
precise number. Every conclusion below is about a factor of 4-5, not 20%.

## Result 1 — the anti-scaling is gone

ms/frame, median of 9. `x` is speedup against that arm's OWN t=1.

  3840x2160 8bpc, 8 tiles (4 cols x 2 rows)

    t     legacy          shard32         shard64
    1    602.3  1.00x     600.7  1.00x    620.6  1.00x
    2    513.0  1.17x     423.2  1.42x    421.6  1.47x
    4    688.3  0.88x     364.7  1.65x    339.6  1.83x
    8   1678.9  0.36x     331.6  1.81x    329.5  1.88x

  3840x2160 10bpc, 8 tiles

    t     legacy          shard32         shard64
    1    675.8  1.00x     722.1  1.00x    749.0  1.00x
    2    557.6  1.21x     516.8  1.40x    511.9  1.46x
    4    700.0  0.97x     452.4  1.60x    407.6  1.84x
    8   1679.6  0.40x     421.5  1.71x    401.2  1.87x

* Decode used to get SLOWER with more threads — 0.36x at t=8, i.e. 2.8x slower
  than single-threaded. It now gets faster at every step: 1.42x, 1.65x, 1.81x.
* At t=8 the sharded tracker is **5.06x faster** than the legacy one (331.6 vs
  1678.9 ms), and **1.55x faster than the best configuration the legacy tracker
  could ship at any thread count** (513.0 ms at t=2).
* Byte-identical output: every cell `md5 all-agree` across arms, and no arm's
  checksum varies with thread count.

Scaling on the non-8-tile vectors, which is where the surprise is:

    vector           arm       t=1    t=2    t=4    t=8
    v4k_1tile 8bpc   legacy   1.00x  1.01x  1.00x  1.00x
    v4k_1tile 8bpc   shard32  1.00x  1.12x  1.12x  1.11x
    v1024     8bpc   legacy   1.00x  0.95x  0.96x  0.95x
    v1024     8bpc   shard32  1.00x  1.36x  1.36x  1.36x

Single-TILE content had no thread scaling at all under the legacy tracker (and
`v1024` actually went backwards). It has some now — 1.36x on `v1024`, 28% off
its wall time. There is post-filter and task-level parallelism there that the
one shared lock was eating even without tile parallelism.

## Result 2 — what it costs, honestly

Ratio to `legacy`, same sweep. Below 1.0 is faster.

    vector              geom            t=1     t=2     t=4     t=8
    v4k_8tile           2160p  8bpc   0.997   0.825   0.530   0.198
    v4k_8tile_10b       2160p 10bpc   1.069   0.927   0.646   0.251
    v4k_1tile           2160p  8bpc   0.981   0.883   0.870   0.883
    v4k_1tile_10b       2160p 10bpc   1.062   0.959   0.956   0.957
    v1024               576p   8bpc   1.022   0.718   0.723   0.717
    v1024_10b           576p  10bpc   1.106   0.872   0.865   0.871
    v256                144p   8bpc   1.045   1.054   1.058   1.068

Two real regressions, both single-thread-shaped:

* **10-bit costs 6-11% at t=1** (`v1024_10b` +10.6%, `v4k_8tile_10b` +6.9%,
  `v4k_1tile_10b` +6.2%), while the 8-bit vectors of the same size are neutral
  to 1.9% FASTER. NOT DIAGNOSED. It is not the block shift — an interleaved
  shift-8-vs-shift-12 A/B on the 10-bit vectors is 1.0015 and 1.0004 at t=1, i.e.
  flat. Cutting to 16 shards only halves it (+4.4% instead of +6.4% on
  `v4k_8tile_10b`), so footprint is part of it but not the whole. The next step
  is to re-run `--features probe-shardsim` on a 10bpc vector: the sizing data
  behind every constant here was taken on 8bpc only.
* **256x144 costs 4.5-6.8% at every thread count.** At that size the whole frame
  is ~1 ms, so per-borrow fixed cost dominates and the extra block/shard
  arithmetic shows up undiluted. Beyond 512p it is invisible.

Peak RSS (`/usr/bin/time -l`, v4k_8tile 8bpc, 2 iterations):

    arm       t=1      t=8
    legacy   75.5 MB  79.4 MB
    shard32  79.5 MB  83.8 MB   +4.0 / +4.4 MB
    shard64  86.2 MB  91.3 MB  +10.7 / +11.9 MB

32 shards x 128 B = 4 KiB per instance that crosses `SHARD_MIN_LEN` (64 KiB);
everything smaller keeps one 128-byte shard.

## Result 3 — against the C reference

dav1d 1.5.4 numbers are QUOTED from `benchmarks/yardstick_dav1d_2026-08-07.meta`
(same host, same vectors, same day, measured by another agent in a separate
sweep). The rav1d-safe column is this sweep. Cross-sweep ratio — treat as
approximate, and note the two sweeps disagree by ~2% on the shared t=1 cells.

  v4k_8tile 8bpc, ms/frame

    t    dav1d1 (tile-threaded)   legacy    shard32   shard32/dav1d1
    1                    246.5     602.3      600.7        2.44x
    2                    125.2     513.0      423.2        3.38x
    4                     65.6     688.3      364.7        5.56x
    8                     34.3    1678.9      331.6        9.67x

    scaling t=1 -> t=8   7.18x     0.36x      1.81x

* The multi-thread gap closes from ~49x to ~9.7x, but it does not close. dav1d
  scales 7.18x on the same eight tiles; we now scale 1.81x.
* The single-thread gap is UNCHANGED at ~2.4x, as expected — the tracker was
  never the single-thread story, and a tracker fix cannot touch it. That gap is
  kernel/codegen, and `benchmarks/neon_tier_isolation_2026-07-28.meta` plus the
  yardstick's Result 4 (zero SIMD-tier spread on aarch64) are where it lives.
* Goal "near rav1d performance" is NOT met and this change was never going to
  meet it. What it does is remove the tracker from the list of reasons.

Against the tracker's own ceiling, which is the fair scoreboard for this work:
`tracker_decomp_2026-08-07.meta` measured an `untracked` build (no tracker at
all, unsound by construction) at 462.2 ms t=1 and 147.5 ms t=8 on this vector.
The sharded tracker lands at 600.7 / 331.6 — so it closes the t=8 gap from
1116.9 ms of tracker overhead to 184.1 ms, about 84%, while the t=1 overhead is
unchanged at ~138 ms. Anti-scaling was the tracker's fault and is fixed; the
flat ~20% per-thread tracker tax is not, and needs a different lever (fewer
borrows, not cheaper ones — the decoder registers ~50 million per 4K frame).

## Result 4 — the shard-count and block-size curves

Screening pass, v4k_8tile 8bpc, 3 reps, before the tracker was boxed (so the
absolute values are ~3% below the final sweep; the shape is what matters):

    shards     t=1     t=8
    legacy    583.9  1120.8
         1    624.0  1318.3     one shard is worse than none of this machinery
        16    567.6   386.4
        32    578.0   333.7     <- default
        64    603.7   304.2
       128    675.9   286.8
       256    743.4   stack overflow in a worker thread

256 shards is 32 KiB of tracker, which overflowed a worker stack while
constructing a `DisjointMut` — that is what forced the tracker behind a `Box`,
and boxing it also dropped `Rav1dTaskContext` from 97 KiB (inline 32-shard
array) to 8.5 KiB, comfortably under its 48 KiB gate.

Block size, at 32 shards, interleaved, median of 3 rounds x 3 reps:

    vector              t    shift 8   shift 12
    v4k_8tile 8bpc      1      605.0      602.4
    v4k_8tile 8bpc      8      349.7      333.9   -4.5%
    v4k_8tile 10bpc     8      433.8      421.2   -2.9%
    v4k_1tile 10bpc     8      640.5      636.9

Shift 12 wins, which contradicts the tile-geometry argument for small blocks (at
4K with 4 tile columns two workers on the same row are 960 bytes apart, so a
4 KiB block puts them in the same shard). The sizing probe explains why: the
peer-collision rate is nearly flat from shift 6 to 12 (0.031-0.040 colliding
peers per add, against 2.07 unsharded), because tile workers are rarely on the
same picture row at the same instant — while the fraction of borrows fitting in
ONE block, and so needing one lock instead of an ordered multi-shard
acquisition, goes 99.875% -> 99.985%. Chosen by measurement, not by the
argument.

64 shards is a real option: it is 1-5% better than 32 at t=4/t=8 (and 5% better
on 10bpc at t=8) for 3% more at t=1 on 8bpc and 4% on 10bpc, plus 7 MB of RSS.
32 is the default because it costs nothing single-threaded on 8-bit content.

## Gates

* **Bit identity.** Full `cargo test --release --no-fail-fast` before (legacy
  arm) and after (sharded default). Every `MISMATCH [tier]: <id> expected= actual=`
  triple extracted from both: **4945 lines / 2845 unique triples each,
  2845 compared, 0 differed.** The triples are the known aarch64 safe_simd drift
  class (569 vector IDs x 5 CPU tiers).
* **Failing-test set did not grow.** 34 failing tests before, 34 after, sets
  IDENTICAL by name. (One earlier iteration DID add a 35th —
  `task_context_stays_stack_light`, from the inline shard array — which is what
  sent the tracker behind a `Box`.)
* **`mt_stress_4k` passes**, covering threads 1 / 2 / 4 / 8 / 16, five trials
  each.
* **Soundness, and it is proven live.** `crates/rav1d-disjoint-mut/tests/
  shard_liveness.rs` checks **331,776 borrow pairs** — starts chosen to straddle
  256 B / 1 KiB / 4 KiB block boundaries, lengths from 1 byte to past the
  wide-path threshold, all four mutability combinations — against the plain
  interval predicate, so a MISSED overlap and a FALSE POSITIVE both fail. Plus
  concurrent overlap/disjoint tests, whole-buffer wide borrows, and open-ended
  `index(..)`. Liveness: rebuilt with `__probe_untracked` (tracking removed),
  **4 of its 5 tests fail**, so the gate is not vacuous. The crate's pre-existing
  `soundness.rs` (27 tests) and the in-module tracker tests also pass.
* **`#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` holds in the safe build.** No unsafe was added
  outside `rav1d-disjoint-mut`, and none of its existing unsafe was widened: the
  new tracker uses exactly the constructs the old one did — `UnsafeCell::get()`
  dereferenced while the relevant lock is held, plus `unsafe impl Send/Sync`.

## Relationship to #450

Not composed. #450 (`perf/audit-445`, open) batches four per-row registrations
under one lock acquisition, recovering the 4-8% #445 cost by cutting lock COUNT.
Sharding attacks a different term — what each lock and record touch costs on a
line shared between tile workers — so they are complementary in principle. They
are NOT measured together here, and the batching's value should be re-measured
on top of sharding before assuming it survives: its win came from amortising
acquisitions of ONE contended lock, and after sharding those acquisitions are
mostly private and mostly uncontended.

## Harness change made here

`scripts/perf/ab_sweep.sh`'s "another sweep is running" guard was a
`pgrep -f '[a]b_sweep.sh'`, which also matches any shell whose command line
merely MENTIONS the script — including `until ! pgrep -f "ab_sweep.sh"` waiter
loops, which match themselves and never exit. Three strays left by another
session were enough to refuse a sweep that had nothing to contend with. It is
now a PID lockfile with stale-holder detection.

## Reproducing

  # arms
  for f in tracker-legacy "" shards-64; do
    CARGO_TARGET_DIR=... RUSTC_WRAPPER=sccache nice -n 19 \
      cargo build --release --example bench_ab_decode ${f:+--features $f} -j 4
  done
  # sweep (vectors: see benchmarks/tile_threading_guard_2026-08-06.meta)
  scripts/perf/shard_sweep.sh <bindir> <vecdir> out.tsv 3
  python3 scripts/perf/ab_report.py out.tsv --baseline legacy

  # sizing probe (throwaway instrumentation, commit a4ebf02)
  cargo build --release --example probe_tracker --features probe-shardsim -j 4
  ./probe_tracker <vec>/v4k_8tile.avif 8 4

